      },
      "lineNbr": 1515,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-06-09T10:41:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We are doing all this after we already mapped the pages for the guest. Isn\u0027t that too late already? The guest may be already accessing these pages (including e.g. writing sensitive data to them etc)?\n\nAlso it doesn\u0027t look like we are even unmapping those pages from the guest here?\n\nAnd even regardless of that, the fact that we are copying pvmfw and mapping pages for the guest without holding host_mmu_lock still feels tricky, and hard to be sure it is gonna be really secure in all cases. (Which seems especially worrisome given how sensitive the pvmfw data is.)\n\nSo why not do it the simple way: after flushing IOMMU TLB, lock host_mmu_lock again, together with locking guest_mmu_lock (just like before this patch), then re-check the host page state, and only after that proceed to copying pvmfw and mapping the pages in the guest MMU (and keep holding both host_mmu_lock and guest_mmu_lock while doing that - again, like before this patch)?",
      "revId": "fe77de2d1d92d36de59b90c9ee06ebcdcf406f9c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    }
